## How this review works

- **Company:** {{ m.company.match_note }}
- **Company type:** {{ m.profile.label }}. {{ m.profile.description }}{% for n in m.profile.notes %} {{ n }}{% endfor %}
- **What was analysed:** {{ m.analysis_type }}. The figures come from the company's filings with the US Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC).
- **Years:** each of the {{ m.years }} fiscal years was analysed on its own, with its own comparison group and the history available up to that year; findings were then matched across years. Each anomaly's estimated effect, investigation and re-check use the most recent year in which it was flagged.
- **Point in time:** each year was analysed with the filings public by the end of its own filing season ({{ m.as_of }} for the last year) (SEC comment letters about each year's annual report, which come later, are the one exception), and each year's figures are taken as first reported, before any later corrections.
- **Comparison group (latest year):** {{ m.peer_method }} Companies: {% if m.peers %}{{ m.peers|map(attribute='name')|join(', ') }}{% else %}none{% endif %}.
- **Legitimate causes checked:** {% if m.explain_conditions %}{{ m.explain_conditions|join('; ') }}{% else %}no acquisition, change in accounting standards or stock split was found in any year reviewed.{% endif %}
- **Data notes:**
{% for n in m.notes %}  - {{ n }}
{% endfor %}- **Filings read:** {% for d in m.documents %}[{{ d.label }}]({{ d.url }}){{ '; ' if not loop.last }}{% else %}none{% endfor %}
- **Limitations:** this review looks for unusual patterns in public filings. It cannot verify cash, assets or transactions, and segment-level figures are not used. The earliest years have less history to compare against, so some tests could not run in them.

